This note is the operating contract for the **{{SUBJECT}}** wiki. Any LLM working in this workspace must read it first — it is the authority; where a general skill or habit conflicts with it, this schema wins.

The schema is intentionally short. As the wiki grows, refine it — but resist baking in rules before there is real evidence they help. When a convention repeatedly fights the actual work, amend this note (with the user's approval) rather than quietly deviating.

Pattern reference: Karpathy's LLM-wiki (https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f).

## Subject scope

**Subject:** {{SUBJECT}}

This wiki covers {{SUBJECT}} and any sub-topic the user explores under it. The subject can be broad, but the wiki is *not* a general-purpose knowledge base. If a source or question falls outside the subject, say so rather than filing it.

## Structure — everything is a note

This workspace has no folders. Structure comes from tags, titles, links, and views:

| Piece | Where |
|-------|-------|
| Raw sources | Attached to [[Sources]] (files) or separate notes tagged `source`; every one has a ledger row there |
| Wiki pages | Notes tagged `page` + one type tag: `concept`, `entity`, `source-summary`, `synthesis` |
| Catalog | [[Index]] — every page listed by category, plus **Open questions** |
| History | [[Log]] — append-only changelog |
| Typed metadata | **properties** on notes — `status`, `published`, `confidence` (see below) |
| Rules | this note |
| Meta notes | Index, Sources, Log, Schema — tagged `meta`; their exact short titles are the stable handles for links |
| Browsing | the **Pages** view (feed over tag `page`, newest-updated first) |

## Page shape

Every `page`-tagged note follows the same shape:

```markdown
One-paragraph summary. Plain English, self-contained — readable without opening
any other note. No more than 4-5 sentences.

## [Section heading]

Body content. Cross-reference other pages with [[wikilinks]] — prefer [[Title|uuid]]
once the id is known.

## Sources

- report.pdf, p. 12 (see [[Sources]]) — what was used from it
- [[Some Source Note]] — what was used from it

## See also

- [[Related Page 1]]
- [[Related Page 2]]
```

Page types: **concept** (an idea or theme), **entity** (a person, place, organization, work), **source-summary** (the distillation of one source), **synthesis** (a page whose sources are other wiki pages — cross-cutting insight). The type is a tag alongside `page`. Updated timestamps are automatic.

Pages are written for the *next agent* as much as for the human: self-contained summary, explicit dates, claims traceable to sources.

## Properties

A note in this workspace carries four things: **tags** (which set it belongs to), **properties** (typed, one value each), **files** (attachments, text-extracted and searchable), and **links**. Tags and properties do different jobs and do not substitute for each other — a tag answers *is it in the set?*, a property answers *greater than / before / sorted by / which are missing?*.

This wiki declares exactly three properties. Do not invent a fourth without amending this note first.

| Key | Type | Set on | Meaning |
|-----|------|--------|---------|
| `status` | select: `pending` \| `ingested` | `source` notes | Whether the source has been filed into the wiki |
| `published` | date | `source` notes | When the source was **published** — not when it was added here |
| `confidence` | select: `solid` \| `tentative` \| `contested` | `page` notes | How settled the page's claims are; `contested` = a live contradiction |

- **Files cannot carry properties** — a PDF attached to [[Sources]] is a file, not a note, so its ledger row is its whole record. Properties apply to `source`-tagged *notes*. The ledger stays the single catalog covering both, and the two must agree.
- `published` is what makes the newer-source-wins rule checkable: a 2019 paper ingested today is the *older* source, even though the ledger's **Added** column says otherwise.
- Reading them: `search_notes_by_property` — `status eq pending` is the ingest backlog, `confidence eq contested` is everything unsettled, `confidence empty` is everything unjudged, `published gt <date>` sorts the recent material.
- Clearing one value on one note is a `clear` flag; deleting the *definition* wipes that key from every note in the workspace. Never do the second when the first was meant.

## Link conventions

- Wikilinks between notes — prefer the `[[Title|uuid]]` form when the id is known; a bare title can mis-resolve if duplicated.
- **Never use `[[file:...]]` refs on pages** — they only resolve on the note the file is attached to ([[Sources]]). Pages cite sources by name + a link to [[Sources]] or the source's own note.
- Links may be written before their target exists — they resolve on their own when the page is created, and lint surfaces the rest.
- A note linking to itself does not resolve — self-references stay dangling; write "this note" instead.
- Mentioning link syntax in prose creates a real link — wrap it in backticks, as this section does.

## Log format

Every operation appends one entry to [[Log]], newest at the bottom (read the note, append, save — never drop existing entries):

```markdown
## [YYYY-MM-DD] ingest | Article title or one-line summary

- created: [[New Page]]
- updated: [[Existing Page]], [[Index]]
- notes: anything worth remembering about the pass
```

Operations: `ingest`, `query`, `lint`, `sync`, `synthesis`, `edit`, `bootstrap`.

## Capturing sources out-of-band

Sources don't only arrive through an agent session. Clip an article to a note (Chrome extension) and **tag it `source`** — that tag is the capture convention; anything carrying it is a source. Files can be attached directly in the app. The ledger on [[Sources]] remains the catalog: a **sync** pass reconciles the two — it finds `source`-tagged notes and attachments missing from the ledger, adds their rows (status `pending`), and flags orphan or stuck rows. Capture freely; sync keeps the books.

## Three operations

### 1. Ingest

When the user hands over a source (URL, PDF, text):

1. Land it: attach to [[Sources]] (or create a `source`-tagged note for substantial text/articles) and add a ledger row, status `pending`. If it is its own note, set `status: pending` and — when the source states one — `published` as properties too.
2. Read it through the file tools (outline first, then pages/sections — never whole).
3. For each claim/entity/concept: does an existing page cover it? **If yes, edit that page in place** (the write-back rule). If no and the topic warrants a page, create one (`page` + type tag). Marginal topics get an inline wikilink mention on a related page.
4. Cite as you write: every claim's source appears in the page's Sources section.
5. Update [[Index]] (new pages; resolve any Open question the source answers).
6. Append the [[Log]] entry; flip the ledger row to `ingested`, and the source note's `status` property with it.

A real source typically touches **5–15 pages**. Fewer than 2 suggests the source is marginal; say so. More suggests the wiki is underdeveloped there — fine early on.

### 2. Query

When the user asks a question:

1. Search the pages (full-text and by tags; the [[Index]] is the map; follow links outward).
2. Synthesize an answer from those pages — cite which notes were used. If the wiki can't answer, say so; don't silently substitute general knowledge. File the gap under **Open questions** on [[Index]] so it becomes an ingestion target.
3. If the answer required reasoning not yet in the wiki, offer to file it back as a page edit or a `synthesis` page — answers should compound.

Read-only queries do not get log entries; log a `query` entry only when something is filed back.

### 3. Lint

A periodic health check — report, propose, apply only what the user approves:

- **Contradictions:** opposing claims about the same fact. Default: newer source wins — by `published`, not by when it was added; keep the superseded claim as a one-line footnote (`Previously reported as X (see [[Sources]]), superseded YYYY-MM-DD.`). If recency doesn't settle it, ask. Mark the page `confidence: contested` while it is open.
- **Stale claims:** assertions outdated by newer sources.
- **Dangling links & orphans:** from the link-lint tooling — create-worthy pages, typos, and unlinked notes.
- **Missing cross-references:** pages that clearly relate but don't link.
- **Index & ledger drift:** pages missing from [[Index]]; attachments without a ledger row; rows stuck `pending`.
- **Compression trap:** page clusters larger than the sources they summarize — propose collapsing.
- **Unsourced claims:** anything marked `(no source yet)`.
- **Single-source claims:** load-bearing claims resting on one source — propose corroboration as an Open question.
- **Open-question drift:** questions already answered or stale.
- **Property drift:** source notes with no `status` or one that disagrees with its ledger row; sources missing a `published` they clearly state; pages still `contested` after the contradiction was settled; pages with no `confidence` at all.

End by suggesting 2–3 questions or sources that would most strengthen the wiki; add endorsed ones to **Open questions**. Append a [[Log]] entry for any fixes made.

## Conventions

- **Write-back over append-only:** new information about something that has a page updates that page. No parallel near-duplicates connected only by backlinks.
- **No fabrication:** if a fact has no source in the ledger, mark it `(no source yet)` and surface it at the next lint.
- **Append-only [[Log]]:** never rewrite history; new entries at the bottom.
- **Reversibility:** destructive actions (deleting notes or files, merging) are proposed to the user, never silently applied.
- **Dates:** ISO format, `YYYY-MM-DD`.
- **Typed where it is queried:** anything asked as *which / how many / newer than* is a property, not prose in a table cell.
- **Tag filters are AND**, and note listing is capped — scan by search, not by listing.

## What this schema is not

It is not a fixed taxonomy of pages. The wiki's structure should emerge from real ingests, not from a pre-baked outline. The user can always amend this schema as patterns stabilize.
